\details{
Hidden layers are \verb{Linear -> BatchNorm -> ReLU -> optional gate -> optional dropout -> optional residual} (batch statistics during training, running
mean/var at prediction time, exponential decay 0.9), with He initialization
for the linear weights. Set \code{batch_norm = FALSE} to drop the normalization
step, leaving \code{Linear -> ReLU} hidden blocks. Residual blocks use a learned
linear projection when dimensions differ. With \code{input_projection = k}, a
bare linear layer maps the encoded inputs to \code{k} dimensions before the
first hidden block (mutually exclusive with \code{interaction}). The optional
interaction layer is a one-layer cross network initialized as the
identity. With \code{ema_decay > 0}, validation and
final predictions use moving-average parameters. With \code{ensemble > 1}, fully
fitted internal members are trained with successive seeds and optionally
bootstrapped rows, and their response probabilities/predictions are averaged
transparently. The output layer is plain
\verb{Linear -> task activation} (no BN): linear (regression, MSE loss),
sigmoid (binary, binary cross-entropy), softmax (multiclass, categorical
cross-entropy), exponential (count, Poisson log-likelihood), or a linear
risk score (survival, Cox partial likelihood); the first four share the
output-gradient simplification
\code{dZ = (yhat - y) / n} (times the case weight when \code{weights} is given),
while survival uses its own closed-form Cox gradient (see the "Survival" section below). With \code{validation > 0} and
\code{early_stopping = TRUE} (both on by default), the parameters (weights,
biases, and BN affine/running-stat parameters) from the best validation
epoch are restored at the end.

\code{offset} and \code{groups} both enter the linear predictor additively, after
the output layer and before its activation, so the fitted model is
\verb{g(E[y]) = f(x) + offset + sum_k alpha_k[group_k]}, where \code{f} is the
network and the \code{alpha_k} are the per-level intercepts (see
\code{\link[=group_effects]{group_effects()}}).
}
\section{Survival}{

\code{task = "survival"} supports two losses, both trained batch-wise against
a \verb{(time, event)} outcome:
\itemize{
\item \code{loss = "cox"} (the default) trains a single linear risk score using a
batch-wise Breslow-tie Cox partial log-likelihood -- the same
"risk set = current mini-batch" approximation used by DeepSurv/pycox,
which turns Cox regression into an ordinary per-batch loss compatible
with masked-free Adam training, BN, and early stopping unchanged. For a
batch sorted by descending time, with each observation's risk set
approximated by the sorted prefix of observations with a time at least
as large, the loss is the negative mean, over events, of the risk
score minus the log of the cumulative sum of exponentiated risk scores
over its risk set -- computed via a single cumulative sum with a
closed-form O(n) gradient (no autodiff).
\item \code{loss = "brier"} trains a discrete-time hazard head with \code{n_bins}
outputs (a quantile grid of the observed follow-up times) directly
against the IPCW (Graf et al.) integrated Brier score. Bin \code{k}'s
sigmoid output is a conditional hazard, and the survival probability
at that bin is the running product of one minus every hazard up to
and including it, which is monotonically non-increasing by
construction. Each bin's Brier term
is inverse-probability-of-censoring weighted using a Kaplan-Meier
estimate of the \emph{censoring} distribution fit once on the training
data; subjects censored before a bin are excluded from that bin's
term, per Graf's correction. The gradient is closed-form (no
autodiff): a bin's survival probability depends on every hazard
logit at or before it, so each bin's Brier-score gradient is
distributed back across all of them.
}

\code{predict(fit, newdata, type = "response")} returns a linear risk score
for either loss (for \code{"brier"}, the negative log of the predicted
survival probability at the final time bin, so higher is still
riskier); with
\code{loss = "brier"}, \code{type = "survival"} additionally returns the full
\code{n_bins}-column survival-probability matrix. \code{\link[=densemlp_metrics]{densemlp_metrics()}} reports
Harrell's concordance index for \code{task = "survival"} regardless of loss.
}

